//! Microcontrollers, with the pin functions and the logic levels of their datasheets (ADR 0046).
//!
//! A part here states the numbers of one datasheet and says which table each came from, as
//! `std::parts::logic` does. Where a level depends on the supply, the part is restricted to the
//! supply range of the row it states, and a board outside that range is refused.

import std::logic::{Levels, Logic}
import std::sources::Rail

/// Microchip's ATtiny13A, an 8-bit AVR microcontroller, in the 8-pin 150 mil (3.9 mm) SOIC that
/// the data sheet calls 8S1 (ordering code `ATtiny13A-SSU`, LCSC C14075). The standard
/// `attiny13a` catalog has that one row; the same die in the 8S2 (208 mil) and the PDIP, QFN
/// and MLF packages has no row and no pin table here.
///
/// Datasheet: Microchip, ATtiny13A Data Sheet, DS40002307A (2021; the complete data sheet,
/// 189 pages, https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/DeviceDoc/ATtiny13A-Data-Sheet-DS40002307A.pdf).
/// The datasheet that LCSC links is the 20-page summary 8126FS (05/12), which has the pinout
/// and no electrical tables, so the numbers below are the complete data sheet's, read from the
/// PDF text. Page numbers are the PDF's printed pages.
///
/// - Functions: Table 10-3, Port B Pins Alternate Functions (p.62), and Figure 1-1 for the
///   pads (pad 1 PB5, 2 PB3, 3 PB4, 4 GND, 5 PB0, 6 PB1, 7 PB2, 8 VCC). A function's kind is
///   written only where the table says which way it goes: `RESET`, `CLKI`, `SCK`, `T0`, `INT0`
///   and the analog inputs `ADCn` and `AINn` are inputs, `OC0A` and `OC0B` are outputs, `dW` is
///   the debugWIRE I/O. `MOSI` and `MISO` carry no kind: the table calls them host data output
///   or client data input (`MOSI`) and the other way round (`MISO`), so the direction is the
///   role the board chooses. `PCINTn` (a pin change interrupt source) carries none either.
///   `GPIO` is Port B's I/O function (section 10, "Ports as General Digital I/O"), a
///   bidirectional pin that the board may choose on many pins at once (`class`); it is the name
///   this library gives that function, because the table gives none. PB5 has no `GPIO`: it is a port pin only when the RSTDISBL fuse is
///   programmed, which this part does not model, so PB5 is the reset input.
/// - Logic levels (Table 18-1, DC Characteristics, TA = -40 C to 85 C, p.123), for a supply
///   of 2.4 V to 5.5 V: an I/O pin reads a low up to 0.3 VCC and a high from 0.6 VCC (the rows
///   "Any Pin as I/O", VCC = 2.4 - 5.5V). The reset pin has its own rows, VCC = 1.8 - 5.5V: a
///   low up to 0.2 VCC and a high from 0.9 VCC (note 4, "Not tested in production"). The
///   datasheet gives other levels for a supply of 1.8 V to 2.4 V (0.2 VCC and 0.7 VCC), which
///   are not stated here, so the part is restricted to 2.4 V to 5.5 V and refuses a supply
///   outside it (the datasheet's operating range is 1.8 V to 5.5 V). The I/O levels are the
///   part's `.levels` (issue #1190), the levels of each of its plain pins `pb0` to `pb4`; the
///   reset pin `pb5` is a `Logic` line with the levels of its own rows.
/// - Outputs state no level. The datasheet gives VOL and VOH only at two supplies and two load
///   currents for each group of pins (PB0 and PB1: 0.8 V and 4.0 V at 20 mA and 5 V, 0.6 V and
///   2.3 V at 10 mA and 3 V; PB2 to PB4: 0.8 V and 4.2 V at 10 mA and 5 V, 0.6 V and 2.5 V at
///   5 mA and 3 V), which one statement cannot hold, as for the output of `LevelTranslator`; a
///   receiver on an output of this part is listed as not evaluated against it, and a board
///   that wants a pair states it for the I/O pins (`.u.levels.voh &= 2.3V at 10mA`, the
///   lower of the two groups at 3 V).
/// - Absolute maximums (section 18.1, p.123): the voltage on any pin but RESET is -0.5 V to
///   VCC + 0.5 V, and on RESET -0.5 V to 13 V. The maximum operating voltage of 6 V, the
///   current per I/O pin (40 mA) and the current in VCC and GND (200 mA) are not stated: the
///   supply is held to 5.5 V, and a board states the currents it puts on the part.
///
/// The supply current is not stated: the datasheet gives it by mode and clock (Table 18-1, p.124),
/// so a board states `.vcc.current` for its use. The levels and the reset line name the supply
/// for their levels and carry nothing, as in `LevelTranslator`. The part is named for the part number because its
/// numbers are that datasheet's. `std/kicad.toml` maps it to the generated footprint
/// `SOIC-8_3.9x4.9mm_P1.27mm` and names no symbol, so the symbol is drawn from the pin table.
/// The catalog is keyed by `.mpn`, so a board that has several rows names the one it buys.
#[kind(ic)]
part Attiny13a {
    .designator &= "U"

    .vcc: Rail
    // Table 18-1, the rows "VCC = 2.4 - 5.5V" of VIL and VIH for any pin as I/O: the levels
    // of the plain pins `pb0` to `pb4`.
    .vcc.voltage >= 2.4V
    .vcc.voltage <= 5.5V
    .levels: Levels
    .levels.reference ~ .vcc
    .levels.vil &= 0.3 * .vcc.voltage
    .levels.vih &= 0.6 * .vcc.voltage
    // The rows "RESET Pin as Reset", VCC = 1.8 - 5.5V: the reset pin has levels of its own.
    .pb5: Logic
    .pb5.reference ~ .vcc
    .pb5.reference.current &= 0A
    .pb5.vil &= 0.2 * .vcc.voltage
    .pb5.vih &= 0.9 * .vcc.voltage

    // Figure 1-1 for the pads, Table 10-3 for the functions, the absolute maximums
    // (section 18.1) in the rows.
    pins "SOIC-8" {
        .pb5.line bidirectional "PB5" "1" fn RESET:input dW:bidirectional ADC0:input PCINT5 abs_min -0.5V abs_max 13V
        .pb3      bidirectional "PB3" "2" fn GPIO:bidirectional class CLKI:input ADC3:input PCINT3 abs_min -0.5V abs_max .vcc.voltage + 0.5V
        .pb4      bidirectional "PB4" "3" fn GPIO:bidirectional class ADC2:input PCINT4 abs_min -0.5V abs_max .vcc.voltage + 0.5V
        .vcc.neg  power         "GND" "4"
        .pb0      bidirectional "PB0" "5" fn GPIO:bidirectional class MOSI AIN0:input OC0A:output PCINT0 abs_min -0.5V abs_max .vcc.voltage + 0.5V
        .pb1      bidirectional "PB1" "6" fn GPIO:bidirectional class MISO AIN1:input OC0B:output INT0:input PCINT1 abs_min -0.5V abs_max .vcc.voltage + 0.5V
        .pb2      bidirectional "PB2" "7" fn GPIO:bidirectional class SCK:input ADC1:input T0:input PCINT2 abs_min -0.5V abs_max .vcc.voltage + 0.5V
        .vcc.pos  power         "VCC" "8"
    }

    .package: Package
    catalog attiny13a by .mpn { .mpn, .package }
}
